  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe cold front is currently pressing through Central NY and it will \nbe through  NE PA before sunrise. Winds turn northwest and become \nbreezy as cold air advection increases. 850mb temperatures fall to \naround +5C by early afternoon, and the boundary layer will see deep \nmixing up to 800mb. This will set the stage for some lake effect \nclouds to develop and perhaps a few light showers this morning off \nof the lower Great Lakes and the Finger Lakes. As drier air mixes in \nthrough the morning and afternoon hours, skies should eventually \nturn mostly sunny. The NW winds will be 8-15 mph, with gusts of 20-\n25 mph expected...a few gusts to around 30 mph will be possible \nacross any exposed higher elevation locations (such as the Pocono \nplateau of NE PA). High temperatures will be cool, only reaching \ninto the 60s this afternoon. \n\nHigh pressure approaches from the northwest this evening and settles \noverhead tonight, rising to 1028 mb overnight. Winds will quickly \ndiminish toward and after sunset, becoming light and variable. Skies \nwill be clear and overnight lows will dip down into the upper 30s to \nmid-40s under a steep low level inversion (850mb temps rise to +9C \novernight). A few of the colder spots could reach the mid-30s, but \nconfidence on any frost formation remains too low at this time.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAfter a cool start Tuesday morning, along with valley fog, it will \nwarm up nicely under sunny skies. A southerly return flow commences \nthrough the day on Tuesday, although there could still be some \nmarine component to the flow off to the north Atlantic. Expect \nplenty of sunshine, dry weather and a large diurnal temperature \nrange, as highs reach into the 70s for most locations. The highly \nanomalous upper level ridge nudges north into Wednesday, as 850mb \ntemperatures reach +14.5C. Can't rule our a stray shower or t'storm \nTuesday night or Wednesday as a weak surface boundary washes out \nover the area. Forcing will be extremely weak with this feature and \nthe main driver of our weather will continue to the rising upper \nlevel heights and thicknesses. While the true core of heat remains \nwell off to the south across the lower Ohio Valley and Tennessee \nValley region, our temperatures will rise into the upper 70s and low \n80s for Wednesday afternoon, which is about 10 degrees above average \nfor Mid September. The warm weather is forecast to continue into \nThursday as an upper level shortwave trough and surface front \napproach. This setup will bring numerous to widespread showers with \na chance for thunderstorms to the area. It will be warm and humid \nwith dew points in the upper 60s to low 70s and temperatures around \n80 degrees. With all the showers and storms around skies are likely \nto be partly to mostly cloudy.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3....\n\nTemperatures gradually cool Friday and especially into next weekend \nas the core of the upper level ridge retrogrades slightly west. This \nwill open the door to a more northwesterly flow locally, with \ngradually falling humidity levels as well. Several front and waves \nmay ride along the northeast periphery of the ridge, keeping shower \nchances in the forecast. Expect high temperatures  in upper 60s to \nmid 70s over the weekend, with overnight lows mainly in the 50s.",
